andards, which govern Wi-Fi technology. MU-MIMO, for instance, was introduced as part of these standards, representing a significant leap in wireless networking capabilities [Data: Relationships (327)]. The broader context of IEEE 802.11 generations underscores the continuous evolution of Wi-Fi, with each generation bringing improvements in speed, range, efficiency, and security [Data: Entities (227)]. This integration ensures that these performance-enhancing technologies are standardized and widely adopted.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Enhancement of Wi-Fi Performance\",\n      \"explanation\": \"MIMO technology directly contributes to improving Wi-Fi speed and efficiency by enabling the simultaneous transmission and reception of multiple data signals [Data: Relationships (136)]. Similarly, Beamforming enhances the quality and efficiency of Wi-Fi communication by focusing wireless signals towards specific devices, leading to stronger connections and less interference [Data: Relationships (138)]. These relationships clearly indicate that MIMO and Beamforming are not just theoretical concepts but practical improvements applied to Wi-Fi networks.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Evolutionary Path of Wi-Fi Technology\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community also encompasses the generational advancements within Wi-Fi, exemplified by Wi-Fi 4 (802.11n). Wi-Fi 4 is recognized as a generation of the IEEE 802.11 standard [Data: Relationships (296)]. This highlights that technologies like MIMO and Beamforming are often introduced and refined across various IEEE 802.11 generations, marking a continuous progression of wireless networking capabilities. The progression from earlier standards to more advanced ones like Wi-Fi 6 and Wi-Fi 7 (802.11be) showcases the sustained development in wireless communication [Data: Entities (227)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Impact on Spectral Efficiency and Network Capacity\",\n      \"explanation\": \"A key outcom